#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/jelly-roll-model.ipynb:9
msgid "Jelly roll model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/jelly-roll-model.ipynb:11
msgid ""
"In this notebook we show how to set up and solve the \"two-potential\" model "
"from \"Homogenisation of spirally-wound high-contrast layered materials\", "
"S. Psaltis, R. Timms, C.P. Please, S.J. Chapman, SIAM Journal on Applied "
"Mathematics, 2020."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/jelly-roll-model.ipynb:13
msgid ""
"We consider a spirally-wound cell, such as the common 18650 lithium-ion "
"cell. In practice these cells are constructed by rolling a sandwich of "
"layers containing the active cathode, positive current collector, active "
"cathode, separator, active anode, negative current collector, active anode, "
"and separator. The \"two-potential\" model consists of an equation for the "
"potential :math:`\\phi^\\pm` in each current collector. The potential "
"difference drives a current :math:`I` through the electrode/separator/"
"electrode sandwich (which we refer to as the \"active material\" in the "
"original paper). Thus, in non-dimensional form, the model is"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/jelly-roll-model.ipynb:16
msgid ""
"\\frac{\\delta^+\\sigma^+}{2\\pi^2}\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}"
"{\\mathrm{d}r}\\left(\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\phi^+}{\\mathrm{d}"
"r}\\right) + 2I(\\phi^+-\\phi^-) = 0,\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/jelly-roll-model.ipynb:18
msgid ""
"\\frac{\\delta^-\\sigma^-}{2\\pi^2}\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}"
"{\\mathrm{d}r}\\left(\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\phi^-}{\\mathrm{d}"
"r}\\right) - 2I(\\phi^+-\\phi^-) = 0,\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/jelly-roll-model.ipynb:20
msgid "with boundary conditions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/jelly-roll-model.ipynb:22
msgid ""
"\\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\phi^+}{\\mathrm{d}r}(r=r_0) = 0, \\quad \\phi^+(r=1) = "
"1, \\quad \\phi^-(r=0) = 0, \\quad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\phi^-}{\\mathrm{d}r}"
"(r=1) = 0.\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/jelly-roll-model.ipynb:24
msgid ""
"For a complete description of the model and parameters, please refer to the "
"original paper."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/jelly-roll-model.ipynb:26
msgid ""
"It can be shown that the active material can be modelled using any 1D "
"battery model we like to describe the electrochemical/thermal behaviour in "
"the electrode/separator/electrode sandwich. Such functionality will be added "
"to PyBaMM in a future release and will enable efficient simulations of jelly "
"roll cells."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/jelly-roll-model.ipynb:38
msgid "Two-potential resistor model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/jelly-roll-model.ipynb:40
msgid ""
"In this section we consider a simplified model in which we ignore the "
"details of the anode, cathode and separator, and treat them as a single "
"region of active material, modelled as an Ohmic conductor, with two such "
"regions per winding. In this case the model becomes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/jelly-roll-model.ipynb:42
msgid ""
"\\frac{\\delta^+\\sigma^+}{2\\pi^2}\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}"
"{\\mathrm{d}r}\\left(\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\phi^+}{\\mathrm{d}"
"r}\\right) + \\frac{2\\sigma^{a}(\\phi^--\\phi^+)}{l\\epsilon^4} = 0,\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/jelly-roll-model.ipynb:44
msgid ""
"\\frac{\\delta^-\\sigma^-}{2\\pi^2}\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}"
"{\\mathrm{d}r}\\left(\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\phi^-}{\\mathrm{d}"
"r}\\right) - \\frac{2\\sigma^{a}(\\phi^--\\phi^+)}{l\\epsilon^4} = 0,\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/jelly-roll-model.ipynb:46
msgid "along with the same boundary conditions."
msgstr ""

